  "description": "L10NSharp.Windows.Forms is a .NET localization library for Windows Forms applications. It collects strings which need localization when your application first runs and saves them in a XLIFF file. It can also dynamically collect strings at runtime.",
  "id": "L10NSharp.Windows.Forms",
  "isPrerelease": true,
  "lastEdited": "2026-11-20T21:52:48.993Z",
  "licenseFile": "LICENSE",
  "licenseUrl": "https://aka.ms/deprecateLicenseUrl",
  "listed": true,
  "packageHash": "j5HJjIaITtw0FbL3++pEsFkvbWrhtkKAE14hThWPYmqqvM2QLPm3e+LoCc6T/2eCwwCqYvoSrSRLtlAotE8o9Q==",
  "packageHashAlgorithm": "SHA512",
  "packageSize": 92266,
  "published": "2026-11-20T21:47:45.7Z",
  "releaseNotes": "Changes since version 11.0.1\n\nChanged:\n- [L10NSharp] Enabled nullable reference types on the core project, with full CS86xx warning cleanup across all layers. `ILocalizationManager.GetLocalizedString` now declares its `defaultText` parameter nullable; the internal `ILocalizedStringCache`/`ILocalizationManagerInternal` interfaces gained nullable annotations and a `where T : class` constraint.\n\nFixed:\n- [L10NSharp.Windows.Forms] Fixed `XliffLocalizationManagerWinforms.ReapplyLocalizationsToAllComponents` (called on every UI language switch) aborting partway through, and continuing to abort on every subsequent switch, when a registered control had been disposed. A disposed control's stale cache entry is now detected and pruned instead of letting its `ObjectDisposedException` propagate; `RefreshToolTips` got the same fix for the same reason. A disposed sub-item owned by a still-live `ILocalizableComponent` container no longer blocks localizing that container's other registered strings either; previously they were skipped on every subsequent switch too. See [#175](https://github.com/sillsdev/l10nsharp/issues/175).\n\nSee full changelog at https://github.com/sillsdev/l10nsharp/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md",
